--- A record written into its own record-or-none field (`n.next = n`) does not build
--- compiled: the emitter moves the record into the link and then writes the field
--- of the moved value.
---
--- Interpreted (crow run -i): prints
---     Node[name = 'a', next = Node[name = 'a', next = Node[name = 'b', next = 0]]]
--- Compiled (crow run): the program does not build:
---     error[E0382]: assign to part of moved value: `n`
---       --> src/main.rs:58:5
---     error: the compiled program does not build — the Rust compiler refused what Crow
---     accepted, a bug in Crow, not in this program (`crow run -i` runs it meanwhile)
--- Expected: the interpreter's output (values are values: the field takes the record
--- as it was before the write, so no cycle is made).
---
--- `crow build --ir` is right — the read of `n` is not a move:
---     n.next = n
--- but the emitter prints
---     n.next = Some(crow_runtime::link::new(n));
--- which moves `n` where the IR copies it (`link::new(n.clone())` would build).

struct Node [name, next]

n = Node['a', Node['b', 0]]
n.next = n
log[n]
